<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Checking in on Health</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in team interactions. Communication: Assist in clear communication of plans and roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve misunderstandings in team discussions about health and balance strategies, using clarifying questions and paraphrasing (e.g., "So, you're saying we should focus on stress management before discussing fitness goals?")
            Can assist in clearly communicating roles and responsibilities during group projects on wellness topics involving health technology (e.g., "You'll handle the section on apps for tracking nutrition, and I will focus on exercise routines.")
            Can use gestures, visuals, and simple language to help clarify complex health-related information in team settings (e.g., drawing a diagram of a balanced diet while explaining portion sizes).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activate prior knowledge and introduce the concept of mediation in health discussions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher projects a short, ambiguous statement related to health and wellness, for instance: "We need to work on fitness for the workshop." Students, in pairs, quickly discuss what they think it means, identifying potential areas for misunderstanding. This activity prompts initial engagement with the need for clarification in communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to resolve a misunderstanding by rephrasing the statement and asking clarifying questions. "When you say 'fitness,' are you referring to physical exercises, or perhaps mental endurance for managing stress? Could you clarify that for me?" The teacher emphasizes polite and formal expressions ("Could you clarify...?", "Are you referring to...?"). The teacher also models the pronunciation of /b/ in "balance" and /p/ in "practice," as well as /s/ in "stress" and /z/ in "exercise."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the goal of asking 'Could you clarify that?'" (To understand better/prevent misunderstanding)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is it important to use formal expressions in health discussions?" (When talking to a professional, in a formal meeting, showing respect)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Checking in on Health." Focus on identifying implicit information and areas requiring mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a group of high school students discussing the initial planning for their "Health and Wellness Workshop" project. The dialogue contains several instances of vague instructions, assumed roles, and potential misunderstandings (e.g., one student says, "Let's track everything," while another mentions "boosting energy," without defining specific methods or responsibilities). The conversation subtly includes examples of present perfect continuous ("We have been discussing the nutrition section, but haven't finalized roles.") and modals for possibility ("We might need to assign specific tasks for tracking habits.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and reformulating information for clearer understanding in collaborative health project planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of this group discussion?" (To plan their health workshop)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students listen again and identify specific phrases from the dialogue that could lead to a misunderstanding and match them with possible clarifying questions (e.g., "Let's track everything" matches "What exactly are we tracking: sleep patterns, activity level, or nutrition?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The group clearly assigned who would be responsible for the 'mental health' station." (False)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation. Reinforce target grammar, vocabulary, and pragmatic functions for effective clarification and role assignment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using present perfect continuous for ongoing health efforts ("I have been exercising daily"), second conditional for hypothetical improvements ("If I had more free time, I would join a yoga class"), and modals of possibility for suggestions ("You might need to drink more water"). Focus on mediation vocabulary (clarify, misunderstand, explain, paraphrase, roles, responsibilities, maintain, improve, track, balance, mindful, essential). Practice pronunciation of /b/ (balance, boost), /p/ (practice, prevent), /s/ (stress, sleep), /z/ (exercise), and sentence rhythm for present perfect continuous ("I <i>have been drinking</i> more water"). Practice giving polite health suggestions and expressing empathy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Clarifying Cloze:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the audio dialogue that contain vague information or potential misunderstandings. They must complete the sentences with appropriate clarifying questions or rephrased statements, using modal verbs for suggestions (<i>might, could</i>) and relevant vocabulary (e.g., "We need a tracker for our workshop." -> "Could you clarify, what kind of ______ (tracker) for which aspect, like activity level or nutrition?"). They also complete sentences demonstrating ongoing health efforts using the present perfect continuous (e.g., "I ______ (practice) meditation to manage stress.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Role Clarification Scenarios:</b> In pairs, students receive cards with incomplete project roles or ambiguous health advice for the workshop (e.g., "Your role is to ensure the team's well-being."). They take turns playing two roles: one student makes an ambiguous statement, and the other uses clarifying questions, paraphrasing, or polite suggestions to make the role/advice clear. They must integrate second conditional sentences ("If I understood correctly, I would focus on creating mindfulness tips.") and expressions of empathy ("That sounds tough, but if you could explain more, I might be able to help."). The teacher encourages careful pronunciation of voiced /b/ in "boost" and voiceless /p/ in "prevent."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "What do you mean by...?" Chains:</b> Students work in groups of three. Student A makes a general statement about the "Health and Wellness Workshop" (e.g., "We need healthy snack ideas."). Student B asks a clarifying question ("When you say 'healthy,' are you referring to low-sugar, nutritious options, or something else?"). Student C paraphrases and adds a polite suggestion using a modal ("So, you're asking if we should suggest things like fruit and nuts? We might also consider recommending a hydration station."). They rotate roles, focusing on precise language, politeness, and sentence rhythm for present perfect continuous ("I have been drinking more water," emphasizing "drinking").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Checking in on Health." Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, specifically applying mediation skills to plan their "Health and Wellness Workshop."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their project groups for the "Health and Wellness Workshop." The task is to finalize the plan for their workshop, ensuring every member clearly understands their roles and the content for each station (nutrition, mental health, fitness). One group member will act as a 'mediator' to facilitate clear communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group planning session. Students are given a checklist of workshop components to finalize (e.g., specific activities for each station, materials needed, assigned presenter for each topic). They must proactively use mediation strategies to resolve any misunderstandings that arise during the discussion about content or responsibilities. They should use clarifying questions, paraphrase information, assign roles clearly using phrases like "You'll handle the section on apps for tracking nutrition, and I will focus on exercise routines," and offer polite suggestions for improvement ("You might want to check reliable sources for those facts."). They are encouraged to use gestures or simple diagrams to clarify complex health information, such as drawing a basic food pyramid while explaining portion sizes for a balanced diet. The teacher moves around, observing and noting effective mediation strategies, correct use of present perfect continuous to describe ongoing planning ("We have been researching apps for self-care."), second conditional for problem-solving ("If we had clearer feedback, we would improve this activity."), and appropriate pronunciation of /b/, /p/, /s/, /z/ in their discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly applies mediation skills to the planning of their "Health and Wellness Workshop," fostering clarity in discussing health strategies and roles within a team, simulating a real-world project development scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' mediation skills during the collaborative workshop planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and peer assessment during a brief presentation of their finalized plan.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their finalized workshop plan (2-3 minutes), highlighting how they ensured clear communication of roles and content. As part of this, they must explicitly state one instance where they used a clarifying question, paraphrasing, or a visual aid to resolve a potential misunderstanding during their planning. Peers use a simple checklist to rate the clarity of the presentation and the evident use of mediation strategies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of clarifying questions and paraphrasing to resolve misunderstandings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear communication of plans and roles using specific language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of gestures, visuals, or simple language to clarify complex information (e.g., explaining "nutrition" or "mindfulness").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent and intelligible pronunciation of target sounds (/b/, /p/, /s/, /z/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated empathy and polite tone in interactions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one specific instance today where you helped someone in your group understand something better about our workshop plan?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about mediating discussions in a team project?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson, self-assessment, and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reflecting on today's activities, what was the most challenging part of mediating discussions or clarifying roles, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will the mediation skills you practiced today be useful in your 'Health and Wellness Workshop' project and other future collaborative tasks?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The activity successfully engaged students in applying mediation skills to a concrete project. Most groups demonstrated effective use of clarifying questions and paraphrasing. Some groups struggled with consistently using formal expressions and empathetic language, which will need more targeted practice. The integration of visuals (diagrams of balanced diet) proved highly effective for clarifying complex health information. Next time, I could provide more structured sentence starters for polite suggestions to further support the sociolinguistic aspect.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies" theme and the "Health and Wellness Workshop" project planning. The next steps for students will be to implement and host their workshops, continuing to apply these communication and mediation skills in a practical, real-world context beyond the classroom for community engagement or school-wide initiatives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>